## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> 1. **Open Interpreter as the Codex execution backend:** Open Interpreter already has `auto_run` mode and supports OpenAI models natively. It can read/write/execute files on your Mac. Should I use it as the primary execution engine, or do you prefer we build raw `subprocess` wrappers ourselves for more control?
> 2. **Skill Priority:** For V1, which skills matter most? My recommendation is: **(a)** Codex/Code Generation + Execution, **(b)** System Monitor, **(c)** File Manager. PII masking and MCP can come in V2. Does that sound right?

---

## Why This Architecture is Scalable

| Concern | Solution |
|---|---|
| **Adding new skills** | Each skill is a Python module in `/skills/`. Drop in a new file, register it → done. No core changes needed. |
| **Swapping the mobile client** | The orchestrator exposes REST + WebSocket endpoints. Telegram, Android, iOS, web — all just clients. |
| **PII/PCI masking** | Plug in [Microsoft Presidio](https://github.com/microsoft/presidio) as a middleware layer that intercepts all inbound/outbound messages and masks sensitive data before it reaches the LLM. |
| **Personalization** | A local SQLite DB stores user preferences, prompt templates, and conversation history. The system prompt is dynamically assembled per-user. |
| **More MCP tools** | The orchestrator can spawn MCP servers (GitHub, Slack, Google Drive, databases) as child processes. Skills can call them via the standard MCP protocol. |
| **Switching LLM providers** | Open Interpreter is model-agnostic. Swap `gpt-4o` for a local Ollama model with one config change. |

---

## Key Open-Source Components

| Component | Repository | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **Open Interpreter** | [OpenInterpreter/open-interpreter](https://github.com/OpenInterpreter/open-interpreter) | Execution engine — gives OpenAI models the ability to read, write, and execute code on your Mac. |
| **Microsoft Presidio** | [microsoft/presidio](https://github.com/microsoft/presidio) | PII/PCI detection and anonymization. Masks credit cards, SSNs, emails, etc. in all messages before they hit the LLM. |
| **Goose (V2 reference)** | [block/goose](https://github.com/block/goose) | Block's open-source agent with deep MCP integration and 70+ extensions. Can be studied for its skill/recipe architecture. |
| **python-telegram-bot** | [python-telegram-bot](https://github.com/python-telegram-bot/python-telegram-bot) | V1 Telegram client library. |
| **FastAPI** | [tiangolo/fastapi](https://github.com/tiangolo/fastapi) | Core orchestrator framework (REST + WebSocket). |

---

## Proposed Implementation Plan

### Phase 1: Project Scaffolding
Create a clean, modular Python project structure:

```
Code-as-a-chat/
├── server/
│   ├── main.py              # FastAPI app entry point
│   ├── config.py            # Environment variables, paths
│   ├── orchestrator.py      # Skill router + intent classifier
│   ├── skills/
│   │   ├── __init__.py      # Skill registry
│   │   ├── codex.py         # Open Interpreter wrapper (OpenAI Codex)
│   │   ├── sysmon.py        # CPU/RAM/Disk monitoring via psutil
│   │   └── filemanager.py   # List/read/write/delete files
│   ├── middleware/
│   │   └── pii_mask.py      # Presidio PII/PCI masking (V2, stub for now)
│   └── db/
│       └── store.py         # SQLite for preferences & history
├── clients/
│   ├── telegram_bot.py      # V1 Telegram bot client
│   └── android/             # V2 React Native / Expo app (future)
├── .env                     # API keys and config
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md
```

### Phase 2: Core Orchestrator (`server/`)
- **FastAPI server** with REST endpoints and a WebSocket endpoint for streaming.
- **Skill Registry:** A simple dict-based registry where each skill registers its name, description, and handler function. The orchestrator looks up the right skill based on intent keywords or an explicit `/command`.
- **Open Interpreter Integration:** Initialize `interpreter` with `auto_run=True` and `model="gpt-4o"`. Route coding-related messages to it. Stream stdout/stderr back to the client.
- **System Monitor Skill:** Use `psutil` to return CPU%, RAM%, disk usage, top processes, and thermal state.

### Phase 3: V1 Client — Telegram Bot (`clients/telegram_bot.py`)
- Use `python-telegram-bot` with long polling.
- Map Telegram commands (`/status`, `/run`, `/files`) to orchestrator REST endpoints.
- Forward free-text messages to the Codex skill via the orchestrator.
- Stream responses back to the Telegram chat.

### Phase 4: Persistence & Always-On
- Use `pm2` or a macOS `launchd` plist to keep the FastAPI server running 24/7.
- Use `caffeinate` to prevent Mac sleep.
- The Telegram bot runs as a second process managed by the same daemon.

### Phase 5 (V2): Android APK
- Initialize a React Native / Expo project.
- Connect to the FastAPI WebSocket endpoint over Tailscale VPN.
- Build a chat UI with skill command palette.
- PII/PCI masking via Presidio middleware activates on all messages.

---

## Verification Plan

### Automated
1. `pytest` for skill unit tests (mock Open Interpreter responses).
2. Lint with `ruff`.
3. Health check endpoint: `GET /health` returns `{"status": "ok"}`.

### Manual (V1)
1. **Codex Test:** Send *"Write a python script that calculates fibonacci numbers and run it"* via Telegram → file should be created on Mac and output streamed back.
2. **System Monitor Test:** Send `/status` → get CPU/RAM/disk stats.
3. **File Manager Test:** Send `/files ~/Desktop` → get a listing of files.
